@page "/docs/typeset"
<PageTitle>Typeset - Blaizio</PageTitle>

@* The typeset guide - and its own best demo: this page's prose is styled by the stylesheet it
   documents (the `typeset typeset-docs` wrapper below, like every guide page). *@
<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Typeset</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">one CSS file</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">zero specificity</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">streaming-safe</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">Typography for the HTML you don't write by hand. One stylesheet, three knobs, wired to your design tokens - from a rendered README to a model streaming markdown into a chat bubble.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <p>Most text a Blazor app renders never passes through a component. Markdig hands you an HTML string, you drop it in with <code>MarkupString</code>, and out come bare headings, paragraphs, lists and tables - no classes, no design review. The usual fix is a pile of element styles written for the blog, rewritten for the docs, rewritten again for the chat transcript, each copy drifting from the last.</p>
        <p>The typeset replaces all of those with one file. Content inside a <code>typeset</code> wrapper gets the site's typography automatically; a small preset class tunes it per context; and because the file is installed source (not a package), changing how your prose reads means editing CSS you already own.</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@HeaderPreset" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>This page is the proof: everything you are reading here is classless markup under one wrapper, and so is every other guide on this site. <a href="typeset">Open the designer</a> to build your own.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Rhythm: three knobs, everything derived</h2>
        <p>Typography systems usually fail by offering too much: a size per heading level, a margin per element pairing, a line-height per breakpoint. Every one of those numbers is a decision, and every decision is a place for two pages to disagree. The typeset works from the opposite end - you set three values, and the arithmetic does the rest:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50 text-start">
                    <BzTableHead>Knob</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>Default</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>What it drives</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell><code>--typeset-size</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>1em</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Body text. Headings, captions, code and table text are em-multiples of it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell><code>--typeset-leading</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>1.75</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Line-height of running prose.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell><code>--typeset-flow</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell><code>1.25em</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The base gap between blocks. A heading takes ~2x of it above and half of it below; list items take a fraction; a rule takes double.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>Change the flow and the headings breathe with it. Shrink the size and the whole hierarchy shrinks in step, because nothing inside is absolute. Fonts follow the same philosophy: <code>--typeset-font-heading</code> and <code>--typeset-font-mono</code> read your existing <code>--font-heading</code> and <code>--font-mono</code> tokens, body inherits the page, and every color comes from the theme - which is why the typeset survives all eight skins, every color preset and dark mode without a line of configuration.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Installation</h2>
        <p><CmdChip Command="blaizio add typeset" /> writes <code>Styles/typeset.css</code> next to your tokens file and records the install in <code>blaizio.json</code> - <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove typeset" /> undoes exactly that, by record. Import it after Tailwind:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@InstallCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>The plain import is deliberate: the file wraps itself in <code>@("@layer components")</code>, so Tailwind's utilities - a later layer - override its rules no matter how it is imported. Keep it after the Tailwind import, though: imported before, the layer order would pin it below preflight. Two behaviors worth knowing follow from the same philosophy: any utility on an element beats the typeset, and links style only when class-less - a classed anchor (a button rendered as an anchor, chrome laid into the prose) is yours to dress.</p>
        <p>Then wrap whatever renders your markdown. <code>typeset</code> switches the styles on; the preset carries your values:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@WrapSnippet" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Building your typeset</h2>
        <p>The <a href="typeset">designer</a> is the fast path to a preset: pick the measure, the three faces and the rhythm knobs, and watch them land on three real sample documents - an article, a technical guide, a chat answer. Lock the knobs you have settled and shuffle the rest; undo and redo cover experiments. Get Code then hands you every piece a project needs: the stylesheet, the webfont link plus <code>--font-*</code> variables for a Blazor host, the preset class, and the wrapper - as numbered steps, or as a single brief you can paste to a coding agent.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Defaults and presets</h2>
        <p>Bare <code>typeset</code> works with no preset at all - the file ships these values:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@DefaultsCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>Two defaults matter more than they look:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Size is <code>1em</code>, not a pixel value.</b> The typeset inherits the type scale of wherever it lands - smaller inside a chat bubble, larger in an article column - and on viewports under 40rem it bumps itself up a touch for arm's-length reading.</li>
            <li><b>No max-width.</b> Line length belongs to your layout, so the stylesheet never sets one. The designer's Measure knob puts a <code>max-w-[70ch]</code> on the wrapper markup instead, where you can see it and change it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Presets are how one app serves several reading contexts. This site runs its docs on one; a chat surface would sit beside it with tighter rhythm:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@PresetCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <CodeBlock Code="@PresetUse" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
        <p>And for a one-off, skip the preset entirely - the knobs are just custom properties, so Tailwind's bracket notation sets them inline:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@InlineOverride" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Presets as reading modes</h2>
        <p>Because the faces are knobs too, a preset can shift the whole character of the content, not just its density. A serif long-read mode and a dense reference mode can share one stylesheet:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@ThemesCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>The same trick answers accessibility: publish a roomier preset and let readers pick it in a settings menu.</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@LargeCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>Dark mode needs nothing - the tokens flip and the typeset follows. If prose feels cramped on the dark surface, open up the leading only there:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@DarkCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Wide tables</h2>
        <p>Markdown tables render as real tables and wrap their cells to fit the column. When wrapping would mangle one - an API matrix, a long option list - wrap it in <code>typeset-scroll</code> and it scrolls sideways inside its own box while the page stays put:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@ScrollSnippet" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
        <p>Emit the wrapper from wherever your pipeline builds tables (a Markdig extension, your renderer's table template). It contains any over-wide block the same way - not just tables.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Escaping the typeset</h2>
        <p>Two escape valves, for two situations:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Override one element.</b> Every typeset rule is wrapped in <code>:where()</code>, which zeroes its specificity - so any utility on any element wins, no <code>!important</code>, no specificity war. Plain CSS with an ordinary selector wins the same way. This is also why Blaizio components dropped into the prose keep their look: their own classes simply outrank rules that weigh nothing.</li>
            <li><b>Exempt a subtree.</b> Mark an element <code>not-typeset</code> (or <code>data-not-typeset</code>) and the typeset skips it and everything inside. The nearest wrapper wins: start a fresh <code>typeset</code> inside an opted-out subtree and styling resumes there - an opted-out card can still hold a typeset chat transcript. This site uses both directions: demo shells and code surfaces opt out, and the chat demos on the Message pages opt back in inside them.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Code="@EscapeSnippet" Numbered="false" Language="html" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Streaming</h2>
        <p>A chat transcript appends blocks while the reader watches. Under most prose stylesheets each arrival restyles the block before it - a last-child margin collapses, a separator jumps - and the message visibly twitches as it grows. The typeset's layout rules are written so that cannot happen:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Nothing looks forward: <code>:last-child</code>, <code>:has()</code> and <code>:empty</code> are banned from layout rules, because what they match changes when content arrives.</li>
            <li>Space flows one way: every gap is a <code>margin-block-start</code>, gated on a preceding sibling, so a new block brings its own spacing and touches nothing above.</li>
            <li>Table separators belong to the row that is already there - an appended row never redraws its neighbour.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Streaming text still grows and wraps normally; the guarantee is only that finished blocks stay finished. Pair it with <a href="docs/components/message">Message</a> and <a href="docs/components/message-scroller">Message Scroller</a> for the rest of a chat surface - both pages carry a live typeset demo: prose in a ghost bubble on one, blocks streaming into a wrapper (watch nothing above them move) on the other.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Relationship to @("@tailwindcss/typography")</h2>
        <p>If you have used the <a href="https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss-typography" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>prose</code> plugin</a>, the typeset will feel familiar - it deliberately keeps the plugin's two best ideas, the zero-specificity guard and the escape-hatch class. It departs everywhere the two tools' goals differ: the plugin ships a beautiful fixed scale as a generated npm artifact; the typeset is a container-relative system, fed by your tokens, tuned per context, safe under streaming, delivered as source you edit.</p>
        <div class="typeset-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th></th>
                        <th>@("@tailwindcss/typography")</th>
                        <th>Typeset</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Scale</td><td>Fixed rem steps (<code>prose-sm</code> … <code>prose-2xl</code>)</td><td>Em-relative to wherever it sits</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Dark mode</td><td>Second palette via <code>prose-invert</code></td><td>Free - the tokens flip underneath it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Theming</td><td>Its own color variables, scale baked in</td><td>Your tokens, plus face and rhythm knobs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Tweaking</td><td>Modifier API (<code>prose-a:</code>, <code>prose-headings:</code>)</td><td>Any utility or plain selector outranks it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Streaming</td><td>Not a design goal</td><td>Append-stable by contract</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Ships as</td><td>npm plugin generating CSS</td><td>A file in your repo, via <code>blaizio add</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private const string HeaderPreset =
        """
        .typeset-docs {
          --typeset-size: 15px;
          --typeset-leading: 1.65;
          --typeset-flow: 1.25em;
        }
        """;

    private const string InstallCss =
        """
        /* Styles/app.css */
        @import "tailwindcss";
        @import "./typeset.css";
        """;

    private const string WrapSnippet =
        """
        <article class="typeset typeset-docs max-w-[70ch]">
            @((MarkupString)Markdown.ToHtml(content))
        </article>
        """;

    private const string DefaultsCss =
        """
        .typeset {
          --typeset-font-body: inherit;
          --typeset-font-heading: var(--font-heading);
          --typeset-font-mono: var(--font-mono);

          --typeset-size: 1em;      /* body font-size */
          --typeset-leading: 1.75;  /* line-height */
          --typeset-flow: 1.25em;   /* space between blocks */
        }
        """;

    private const string PresetCss =
        """
        .typeset-docs {
          --typeset-size: 15px;
          --typeset-flow: 1.5em;
        }

        .typeset-chat {
          --typeset-flow: 1em;
          --typeset-leading: 1.6;
        }
        """;

    private const string PresetUse =
        """
        <article class="typeset typeset-docs">@pageHtml</article>
        <div class="typeset typeset-chat">@messageHtml</div>
        """;

    private const string InlineOverride =
        """
        <article class="typeset [--typeset-flow:1.75em] [--typeset-size:17px]">...</article>
        """;

    private const string ThemesCss =
        """
        /* Long reads: serif faces, generous rhythm. */
        .typeset-longread {
          --typeset-font-body: var(--font-lora);
          --typeset-font-heading: var(--font-lora);
          --typeset-size: 18px;
          --typeset-leading: 1.9;
          --typeset-flow: 2em;
        }

        /* Reference: compact, information-dense. */
        .typeset-reference {
          --typeset-size: 13px;
          --typeset-leading: 1.55;
          --typeset-flow: 0.875em;
        }
        """;

    private const string LargeCss =
        """
        .typeset-comfort {
          --typeset-size: 17px;
          --typeset-leading: 2;
          --typeset-flow: 1.75em;
        }
        """;

    private const string DarkCss =
        """
        .dark .typeset {
          --typeset-leading: 1.9;
        }
        """;

    private const string ScrollSnippet =
        """
        <div class="typeset-scroll">
            <table>...</table>
        </div>
        """;

    private const string EscapeSnippet =
        """
        <div class="typeset typeset-docs">
            <p>Typeset prose.</p>
            <p class="text-lg text-primary">One element, overridden by utilities.</p>
            <BzCard Class="not-typeset">A whole subtree, exempt.</BzCard>
        </div>
        """;
}
